-### Planned upgrade: higher-current bus with regen absorption
-
-Under consideration 2026-09-20: replacing the `S-360-36` with a **48 V 25 A
-(1200 W)** supply. Recorded here as a design note, not a committed change.
-
-**What it fixes.** The `S-360-36`'s 10 A leaves no current margin against the
-estimated 9.6-11.2 A four-drive draw. 25 A removes that problem completely, and
-higher bus voltage raises available torque above 300 RPM, which is where this
-machine's 508 RPM rapids sit.
-
-**What it costs.** The CL57T ceiling is a hard 50 V. At 48 V nominal only 2 V of
-regen headroom remains, against 14 V at 36 V today.
-
-#### Why regen matters here despite the machine's mass
-
-The intuition that a heavy lead-screw machine cannot generate much regeneration
-is correct at present speeds, but the margin is thinner than it feels and it
-scales badly.
-
-Order-of-magnitude, assuming ~40 kg moving mass and a 135 mm NEMA 23 rotor:
-
-| Source | Energy at 2540 mm/min |
-| --- | --- |
-| Linear kinetic (mass at 42 mm/s) | ~0.04 J - negligible, as expected |
-| Rotor kinetic, per motor at 508 rpm | ~0.11 J |
-| X plus the ganged Y pair stopping together | **~0.34 J** |
-
-Against roughly 3000 uF of existing bus capacitance, a 48 to 50 V excursion
-absorbs about 0.29 J. Friction and winding resistance burn part of the total
-before it reaches the bus, so 100 IPM is probably survivable - but it is the same
-order of magnitude, not a comfortable ratio.
-
-**The scaling is the real argument.** Regen energy goes with velocity squared:
-
-| Rapids | Approx. regen | vs ~0.29 J headroom at 48 V |
-| --- | --- | --- |
-| 100 IPM | ~0.37 J | marginal |
-| 150 IPM | ~0.83 J | over |
-| 200 IPM | ~1.5 J | well over |
-
-So 48 V untrimmed has just enough headroom at today's speeds and loses it at
-exactly the speeds the extra voltage was bought to reach.
-
-#### Design if the upgrade proceeds
-
-1. **Trim the supply to about 44 V.** Confirm a `V-ADJ` pot exists before
-   purchase; skip any fixed-output 48 V unit. This keeps the full 25 A headroom
-   and most of the torque gain while restoring roughly 6 V of regen margin.
-2. **Add bus capacitance, about 10,000 uF at 63 V.** A switch-mode supply is
-   unidirectional and absorbs no regeneration, so capacitance is the only place
-   for that energy to go. At 44 V with 6 V of rise, ~13,000 uF total absorbs
-   about 3.7 J - comfortably above the 200 IPM case. Rate it 63 V, not 50 V.
-3. **Inrush limiter, mandatory.** 13 mF presents as a short at power-on and will
-   weld relay contacts, trip breakers, or damage the supply. An NTC inrush
-   thermistor in series with the bus feed, roughly 5-10 ohm and 20 A rated, is
-   sufficient at this scale.
-4. **Bleeder resistor, mandatory.** 13 mF at 48 V stores about 15 J and stays
-   charged after switch-off - enough to vaporise metal at a dropped tool. Fit a
-   permanent ~2.2 kohm 5 W resistor across the bank: about 1 W continuous, drains
-   in roughly two minutes. **Meter the bus before touching anything**, the same
-   discipline a VFD demands.
-5. Connect the bank at the distribution point, across the same node as the four
-   `P4` feeds. Watch polarity; a reversed electrolytic on a 25 A supply fails
-   violently.
-
-**A brake chopper is not needed.** Those dissipate kilojoules from spindle-class
-braking. This machine's regeneration is single-digit joules, which capacitance
-absorbs directly.
-
-#### Consequences to carry forward
-
-- Per-drive branch fusing becomes essential rather than good practice: 1200 W
-  puts far more fault energy on the rail. Size fuses to the drive, not the supply.
-- Measure the backplate before ordering. 1200 W enclosed supplies are physically
-  large and the plate is already populated.
-- Drives run hotter on a higher bus. The 40 C limit and 30 mm spacing from the
-  CL57T manual become real constraints.
-- `S1` stays at `4`. The current setting is independent of bus voltage.
-- Re-run the rapids and acceleration decision after the change, since raising
-  them is the reason for the upgrade and regen scales with the square of speed.
+- Select any replacement only after a measured load/regeneration study; a
+  current rating alone does not establish suitability.
+
+**With supply power isolated, verify the actual input selector and nameplate
+against the measured facility service.** Do not assume a 110 V feed or move a
+selector while energized.
+
+### Higher-Current / Higher-Voltage Supply Proposal - HOLD
+
+An earlier design note proposed a 48 V / 25 A supply with an added capacitor
+bank. **No upgrade, trim voltage, capacitor bank, inrush limiter, bleeder or
+braking circuit is released for construction.** The previous mass, rotor
+inertia, capacitance and regeneration figures were assumptions. They do not
+bound actual deceleration, gravity-loaded Z energy, supply behavior or faults.
+The claim that a brake chopper is unnecessary is withdrawn.
+
+Before selecting any replacement, document the actual four-drive load, supply
+transient response/overvoltage behavior, returned energy for the full operating
+envelope including Z descent, drive bus limit, branch protection and stop
